  },
  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; the full paper and supplementary material are present.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Extends model-scaling analysis to supervised scene text recognition, an image-based task, and reports benchmark accuracy across encoder and decoder sizes and training-data volumes.",
    "gate_note": "The scaling sweep measures average word accuracy, not held-out cross-entropy, and does not fit or test a power-law form. The results are recorded as findings about scaling in STR, but do not directly measure any listed claim version. Cited scaling-law results from other work and the paper's CSD and decoder-architecture comparisons are not included as scaling-law results.",
    "test_reason": "This is a scene-text-recognition paper. It sweeps encoder and decoder sizes and reports benchmark word accuracy (Table 2, Fig. 1), and scales data for one model (Table 4). It fits no power law, measures no held-out loss, does no extrapolation or compute-optimal allocation, and makes no fixed-parameter shape comparison. It cites Kaplan et al. and uses 'scaling laws' loosely ('more favorable scaling laws'). Accuracy points on a log-parameter axis cannot show whether the power-law form holds, so V5 is not tested. V6 concerns language-model downstream predictability, which is also not measured. The reader's 'tests/T3' contradicts its own finding that every version is not_tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors conclude that decoder scaling gives larger, more parameter-efficient STR accuracy gains than encoder scaling, contrary to prior work. They find that encoder scaling can hurt on limited, noisy real data. They also propose Cloze Self-Distillation against label noise and a differential cross-attention decoder, reaching SOTA on 10 of 11 benchmarks with fewer parameters.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study evaluates supervised STR encoder-decoder scaling and word accuracy, rather than language-model pretraining loss or the original scaling-law fitting procedure.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported scaling outcome varies with encoder/decoder configuration and training-data volume; on Real data, scaling ViT-S to ViT-B with smaller decoders can reduce accuracy, while on RBU the larger encoder improves performance across decoders.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Encoder scaling gains depend on data volume. ViT-S to ViT-B lowers Real-data accuracy with smaller decoders, while on RBU it raises accuracy with every decoder. Decoder scaling helps for every encoder.",
        "proposed_claim": "In supervised STR, benchmark accuracy gains from scaling encoder versus decoder depend on component and training-data volume.",
